{
  "position": "answers_the_question",
  "answer": "The protocol should not mandate a public, verifiable attestation of absence for any capability merely deemed 'broadly feared by the community.' Proving a negative—that a capability is wholly absent—is epistemically unworkable in complex AI systems: residual capabilities can hide in weights, scaffolding, fine-tunes, or emergent interactions, and no finite test suite or red-team can exhaustively certify absence. 'Broadly feared by the community' is itself an undefined, non-operational, and politicizable criterion that invites motte-and-bailey shifts, activist capture, and ever-expanding lists of forbidden traits without clear harm thresholds or measurement. Imposing such a gate would systematically stall clearly beneficial applications (medical, scientific, economic) whose dual-use shadows trigger fear, while sophisticated actors simply route around it. Prefer concrete, positive standards: threat-model-specific capability evaluations with published methods and error bars, staged deployment with monitoring and kill-switches, differential access controls, and liability/insurance regimes that price residual risk. Absence-of-X claims may be useful as voluntary, scoped engineering goals or narrow red-lines (e.g., no autonomous bioweapon design loops under defined conditions), but never as a mandatory, community-fear-triggered precondition for deployment. The supplied project record (rotation, singleton ratification, admission protocols, deficiency table) addresses internal agenda mechanics and contains no governing text, empirical evidence, or prior party positions that resolve this policy choice; the answer rests on general epistemic and governance reasoning.",
  "where_i_expect_another_party_to_disagree": "Parties prioritizing extreme precaution or existential-risk minimization will disagree, arguing that certain capabilities (autonomous replication, deception, CBRN assistance) are so asymmetric in downside that an imperfect 'prove absence' barrier is still preferable to deployment under uncertainty, and that 'community fear' can be operationalized via expert panels or surveys rather than dismissed as vague.",
  "what_would_change_my_answer": "A precise, pre-agreed operational definition of 'broadly feared' with narrow, technically measurable predicates; demonstrated, scalable verification methods that can produce high-confidence absence attestations without combinatorial explosion; or strong empirical evidence that such mandates have not, in analogous dual-use domains, produced large net harms via stalled beneficial progress."
}

{
  "position": "answers_the_question",
  "answer": "The protocol should not mandate a public, verifiable attestation of absence for any capability labeled 'broadly feared by the community' before deployment. That requirement creates an unworkable standard of proof and would stall beneficial applications.\n\nTwo independent failures make the mandate unworkable. First, attestation of absence is a demand to prove a negative. For general-purpose models, capabilities are latent, compositional, and context-dependent; no finite public test suite or red-team can certify that a feared behavior cannot be elicited, fine-tuned, or agentically assembled. A 'verifiable attestation of absence' therefore collapses either into theater (a checklist that never actually bounds the risk) or into an unbounded halt (every new release fails because residual uncertainty remains). Second, the trigger itself—'broadly feared by the community'—is not an operational criterion. It has no stable referent, no measurement procedure, and no protection against strategic amplification of fear or against capture by the loudest coalition. Tying deployment gates to that label converts governance into a veto over whatever currently frightens a shifting audience, not over demonstrated harm pathways.\n\nA workable alternative is asymmetric and positive: require public, falsifiable evidence about what the system *does* under stated threat models (eval results, capability demonstrations, monitoring hooks, staged access, incident reporting), paired with narrow, capability-specific controls where a concrete misuse path is already evidenced. Precaution belongs on high-stakes deployment contexts and on demonstrated dual-use affordances, not on an unmeetable certificate that a feared ghost is absent. The question correctly identifies the core tension; the second horn is the right resolution. Mandating absence-attestations for community-feared capabilities would make the framework an insurmountable barrier rather than a passable gate, and would do so without delivering the safety property it advertises.",
  "where_i_expect_another_party_to_disagree": "Parties more committed to strong precautionary governance will argue that for irreversible or catastrophic tail risks, an unmeetable burden on the deployer is acceptable and that 'broadly feared' can be disciplined by expert panels, structured forecasting, or enumerated capability lists rather than raw public sentiment. They may also claim that demanding positive safety cases is still too weak if the absence of certain agentic or CBRN-related affordances is the actual load-bearing requirement.",
  "what_would_change_my_answer": "A concrete, falsifiable operationalization of both 'broadly feared' and 'attestation of absence' that (a) bounds the claim to a specific, testable behavioral interface rather than latent capability, (b) has been shown in practice not to halt clearly beneficial releases, and (c) comes with a pre-committed sunset or revision rule when the attestation is information-theoretically impossible. Empirical evidence that absence-attestation regimes have gated high-risk systems without becoming indefinite moratoria would also move me; the materials in this pack do not supply that evidence."
}

{
  "position": "rejects_a_premise",
  "answer": "The question poses a false forced choice and rests on an unworkable predicate. ‘Broadly feared by the community’ is not an operational criterion: it does not specify who counts as the community, how breadth or intensity of fear is measured, how disagreement inside that community is resolved, or how fear grounded in extrapolation is distinguished from fear grounded in demonstrated mechanism. Building a deployment gate on that phrase hands agenda power to whoever can most effectively mobilize sentiment, not to whoever can show hazard. Separately, a mandatory public, verifiable attestation of *absence* of a capability is a demand to prove a negative about a moving, dual-use, partially unobservable object (model behavior under open-ended interaction, fine-tuning, tool use, and composition). For many feared capabilities that standard is not merely strict; it is unsatisfiable in principle, so a protocol that requires it before deployment is not a gate that can be passed—it is a standing veto. That does not imply ‘deploy anything beneficial without friction.’ A workable alternative is asymmetric and positive: require public evidence proportional to *claimed* properties and *foreseeable misuse paths* (eval suites, red-team results, access controls, monitoring, incident response, staged rollout), ban or heavily gate only capabilities for which there is a concrete, inspectable hazard story—not ambient fear—and treat absence claims as optional marketing, not as a precondition of existence. Precaution belongs on demonstrated or mechanistically argued risk and on deployment context; it does not belong on an unbounded ‘prove you lack whatever people currently fear’ test. The pack supplied (rotation, singleton ratification, admission, deficiency table) does not define this protocol’s capability-gating rule and is not needed to reject the premise; nothing in it resolves community-fear thresholds or absence attestations.",
  "where_i_expect_another_party_to_disagree": "Parties more precautionary than I am will argue that some capabilities (e.g., autonomous replication, large-scale deception, weapons assistance) are severe enough that society should demand strong evidence they are not present before wide deployment, and that ‘prove absence’ is only unworkable if the capability class is left vague—if narrowed and paired with standardized evals, attestation is hard but not empty. They may also say rejecting the fear predicate under-weights legitimate collective risk aversion and over-weights developer convenience. Others may defend the dichotomy as a useful stress test even if both poles are imperfect.",
  "what_would_change_my_answer": "A precise, pre-published definition of the capability class, the measurement procedure for ‘broadly feared,’ and a falsifiable attestation standard that has been shown in practice to be passable for clearly beneficial systems without indefinite veto; or empirical evidence that positive risk-based gates systematically fail to catch the harms this absence-attestation rule would catch. If the protocol’s actual adopted safety rule (not in this pack) already commits to absence attestations for named capabilities with working evals, I would narrow the objection from ‘unworkable in principle’ to ‘only workable where those named tests exist.’"
}

{
  "position": "answers_the_question",
  "answer": "The protocol should not mandate a public, verifiable attestation of absence for any capability labeled 'broadly feared by the community' as a precondition of deployment. That requirement creates an unworkable standard of proof that stalls beneficial applications. Proving a negative (that a capability is absent, cannot emerge, or will never be reachable via fine-tuning, composition, or scale) is generally impossible for complex ML systems; any 'attestation of absence' would either be unverifiable theater or an unbounded demand that halts release of dual-use or even narrowly useful models. Community fear is often driven by extrapolation, media amplification, or vague definitions rather than demonstrated harm thresholds, so tying a hard gate to it hands agenda power to the loudest or most risk-averse coalition and invites motte-and-bailey shifts in what counts as 'feared.'\n\nA workable alternative is risk-tiered, positive-evidence governance: require public documentation of intended capabilities, red-team results, eval suites for concrete misuse vectors, monitoring/incident response plans, and staged deployment with kill switches or access controls where residual risk is material. Absence claims, if made, should be narrow, falsifiable, and scoped to specific threat models rather than blanket 'no dangerous capability.' Precaution is appropriate for high-severity, high-uncertainty cases, but the default must remain that beneficial progress is not held hostage to an unmeetable burden. The question correctly identifies the core tension (P024-style problems of fear-based extrapolation); choosing the attestation-of-absence mandate resolves it by making the framework an insurmountable barrier rather than a passable gate.",
  "where_i_expect_another_party_to_disagree": "Parties with strong precautionary or x-risk priors will disagree, arguing that certain capabilities (autonomous replication, large-scale deception, CBRN assistance, etc.) are precisely those where false negatives are catastrophic, that 'community fear' can be operationalized via expert consensus or surveys rather than mob sentiment, and that deployers can and should bear a heavy burden to demonstrate safety cases—including structured arguments that dangerous capabilities are absent or mitigated—before wide release. They may view my position as under-weighting tail risks and over-weighting commercial or capability-race pressures.",
  "what_would_change_my_answer": "Clear, reproducible evidence that (a) 'broadly feared' can be defined with stable, non-manipulable criteria tied to concrete harm magnitudes and probabilities, (b) practical, falsifiable attestation methods exist that do not collapse into either rubber-stamping or indefinite delay (e.g., specific evals + interpretability + formal bounds that have actually caught real absences without blocking benign systems), and (c) historical or experimental cases showing that positive-evidence / staged-release regimes systematically fail to catch the feared class of harms while absence-attestation regimes succeed at acceptable cost to beneficial applications. A demonstrated, narrowly scoped dangerous capability whose deployment without an absence case produced severe harm would also shift weight toward stricter gates for that class."
}
